//Virtual Destructor
A virtual destructor is a destructor declared with the virtual keyword in the parent class.

It is important when we delete a child object through a parent pointer.

Example:

class Animal {
public:
    virtual ~Animal() {
        cout << "Animal destructor";
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    ~Dog() {
        cout << "Dog destructor";
    }
};

Now:

Animal* a = new Dog();
delete a;

Because the parent destructor is virtual, destruction happens correctly:

Dog destructor
Animal destructor
Why do we need it?

Without a virtual destructor, deleting a derived object through a base-class pointer can result in the derived destructor not being called, which can cause improper resource cleanup.

Simple rule

If a class is intended to be used as a base class polymorphically, its destructor should generally be virtual.

Simple definition:

Virtual destructor = a destructor that ensures the correct child and parent destructors are called when deleting an object through a parent pointer.